    <h1>Chilli Powder Colour Change &amp; Storage</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Colour is not permanent. Chilli powder fades over time — faster after sterilisation — and
      sweet paprika can darken. Both are normal pigment chemistry, and four storage conditions keep
      colour stable longest.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Chilli powder lightens and sweet paprika darkens over time, but keeping moisture under 13% in cool, dark, sealed storage slows both. Faster change follows high-temperature sterilisation; both changes are normal carotenoid chemistry, not a defect.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why chilli powder changes colour</h2>
    <p>
      Chilli and paprika get their red colour from carotenoid pigments, which are sensitive to light,
      heat and oxygen. Two changes matter in practice: chilli powder <strong>lightens</strong> as the
      red carotenoids oxidise, and sweet paprika can <strong>darken</strong> when its higher sugar
      content browns under heat. Neither means the product is spoiled — but the colour it ships with
      is not the colour it will show after months on a shelf.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Product</th><th>Change</th><th>Trigger</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Chilli powder</strong></td><td>Lightens (fades)</td><td>Time; accelerates after high-temperature sterilisation</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sweet paprika</strong></td><td>Darkens</td><td>High-temperature sterilisation, then time</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The storage checklist</h2>
    <p>Four conditions, in order of importance:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cool</strong> — heat accelerates colour loss</li>
      <li><strong>Dry</strong> — keep moisture under 13%</li>
      <li><strong>Dark</strong> — light breaks down carotenoids</li>
      <li><strong>Sealed</strong> — limit oxygen, which drives oxidation</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">When sterilisation is required</h2>
    <p>
      Some buyers require high-temperature sterilisation for microbial control. If so, expect the
      colour to shift — fading for chilli, darkening for sweet paprika — and build it into your
      specification by setting a higher starting ASTA. See the full
      <a href="https://docs.drychillis.com/colour-storage/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">colour &amp; storage reference</a> in the Knowledge Hub.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The chemistry in one paragraph</h2>
    <p>
      The red of chilli and paprika comes from carotenoids — chiefly <strong>capsanthin</strong> and
      <strong>capsorubin</strong>, with smaller amounts of beta-carotene and zeaxanthin. These pigments
      have long chains of conjugated double bonds, which is exactly what makes them both deeply
      coloured and reactive: light, heat and oxygen attack those bonds, breaking the chromophore and
      fading the colour. Sweet paprika has a higher natural sugar content, so heat triggers Maillard
      browning — the sugars darken — on top of any pigment loss. That is the whole story: oxidation
      fades, sugar darkens.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How colour is measured (ASTA)</h2>
    <p>
      Chilli and paprika colour is quantified on the <strong>ASTA scale</strong> — named after the
      American Spice Trade Association — which measures the extractable red pigment. Sweet paprika for
      colouring typically runs from roughly 100 to 240 ASTA depending on variety and grade; higher
      ASTA means more red colour per unit of powder. When sterilisation is unavoidable, buyers
      compensate by specifying a higher starting ASTA so the shipped colour still lands on target
      after the fade. See <a href="https://docs.drychillis.com/asta-shu-explained/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ASTA colour explained</a> in the Knowledge Hub.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Moisture is the silent variable</h2>
    <p>
      Colour is the visible problem, but moisture is the underlying risk. Chilli and paprika powder
      should stay under <strong>13% moisture</strong> — above that, clumping begins, microbial growth
      becomes possible, and oxidation accelerates. Moisture and water activity (<em>a<sub>w</sub></em>),
      not just water content, are what determine shelf stability. Sealed packaging with a low
      oxygen headspace protects both the colour and the moisture spec at once.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note" style="margin-top:10px;">Values are representative ranges (MEDIUM confidence) that vary by variety and origin. Confirm your target SHU, ASTA, moisture and mesh with us. 复核 2026-09-09.</p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why does chilli powder lose colour over time?</h3>
      <p>Chilli powder lightens gradually at room temperature as its red carotenoid pigments degrade, and the change is faster after high-temperature sterilisation. Cool, dry, dark, sealed storage slows this.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Does colour change mean the chilli has spoiled?</h3>
      <p>Not necessarily. Gradual lightening in chilli powder and darkening in sweet paprika are normal pigment behaviour. Check moisture and odour to tell normal colour shift from actual spoilage.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How do I keep chilli powder colour stable?</h3>
